## 1.2.25
- Scrolling: cache conversation layout height outside animation frames, use finger travel for swipe distance and make the glide settle faster without removing its easing.
- Dictation: microphone icon redrawn as a solid microphone with a cleaner cradle; the red listening state remains.
- Dictation: the watch's keyboard language now selects English (en-US) or Egyptian Arabic (ar-EG) explicitly.

## 1.2.24
- Keyboard: the empty send slot is now a phone-microphone dictation button; speech appears in the editable draft, and typing switches the same slot back to the blue send button.

## 1.2.23
- Conversation: the black rim round the app sign is smooth now. It was drawn in whole pixels, so its edge looked stepped, and Messenger's came out lumpy because dark corner pixels of its picture were counted as part of the sign. The rim now follows the real shape evenly (tail included) and its edge blends into the bubble colour under it. The round sign of other apps got the same soft edge.

## 1.2.22
- Conversation: the app sign at the top has no black circle behind it any more. It has a black outline that follows its own shape (the tail included), so the bubbles scrolling under it show right up to its edge.

## 1.2.21
- The app sign and the Message box now float over the bubbles (each with a black rim); the bubbles scroll under them all the way to the screen edge instead of being cut off above and below.
- Less lag: the small WhatsApp sign is pre-drawn (it was filtered again on every frame), and the glide takes the same time however long a frame needs.

## 1.2.20
- WhatsApp icon redrawn: smooth round edges, a clean tail and a black rim around it like the others (it was a jagged bitmap that touched the edge).

## 1.2.19
- Bubbles stick to the round wall: theirs to the left side, mine to the right side, and they follow the curve of the wall as you scroll (no more middle lane).
- Smoother glide: smaller steps, more frames.

## 1.2.18
- Curved list: in the messages screen the bubbles follow the round screen (all of them now, also the middle ones), and the gliding is quicker.
- Drawing is faster: the round-screen edge is looked up from a table instead of being worked out for every row.
- The list of chats (WhatsApp, Messenger...) is unchanged: no curve, no slide.

## 1.2.17
- Conversation: bigger bubbles. Plain English messages use the big font (32 px, was 27), with more room round the text and a wider bubble; messages with Arabic letters keep the Arabic-capable font, a little more spaced.
- Conversation: your own reply no longer comes back as a message from them. The chat app puts your reply in its notification and the phone sent it on as a new message, so your "hi" showed up on the left as theirs. For 8 seconds after you send, a message from that chat with the same text (or "You: text") is taken for your own reply and left out. It counts once, and a message with other words, or from another chat, is shown as usual.

## 1.2.16
- Font: the T font (fixed English UI text and single key letters) no longer carries its own Arabic table — it was an exact duplicate of the one in B, which is what Arabic text actually uses. Frees about 18.7KB.
- Word/suggestion lists trimmed further.

## 1.2.15
- Shrank the word lists (suggestions) so the app fits in the image size again — it was overflowing by about 30KB after Arabic was added.

## 1.2.14
- Conversation: every bubble has a pointed tail at its bottom corner, on the left for theirs and on the right for yours.

## 1.2.13
- Message cards: shorter, so the bottom gap matches the top; the app sign gets a black cut-out that follows its outline (the WhatsApp and Messenger tails included), half on the card and half off.

## 1.2.12
- Conversation: the app sign at the top is smaller (44 px) and the bubbles start higher, so more messages fit.

## 1.2.11
- Conversation: the sender's name is smaller than the text, the bubble corners are rounder, your replies are a normal grey with white text, and bubbles from other apps (SMS and so on) are light grey.

## 1.2.10
- The conversation screen is now a chat: their messages are bubbles on the left with the name (green WhatsApp, blue Messenger, light blue Telegram, dark grey for other apps), your replies are light grey bubbles on the right, and the reply button is a "Message" box (tap it for the keyboard). Replies you send from the watch stay in the conversation.

## 1.2.9
- WhatsApp icon: it had empty margin around it, so it looked smaller than Messenger and Telegram. Trimmed and scaled to the same full size.

## 1.2.8
- Keyboard: the text line and the suggestion row moved up about 28 px, and the keys use the room: taller keys, and the top row a little narrower so it stays on the round screen.

## 1.2.7
- Reply button: taller, with a larger "reply".
- Keyboard: bigger letters; a smaller space bar; bigger send and delete buttons. (The unused arrow tile next to the suggested words was already removed in 1.2.6.)

## 1.2.6
- Chats are told apart by the phone's id only. Before, chats whose names were replaced by dots (Arabic names) were taken for one chat, so only one WhatsApp chat showed.
- Suggested words: while you type a word, the row above the keys offers words that continue it (ho -> home); tap one to finish the word and add a space.

## 1.2.5
- Keyboard: wider keys, using the room the round screen has at each row. Quick replies: wider fit, and "Ok / On my way / Thanks!" show even before the phone sends its own.

## 1.2.4
- Messages from the same chat now pile up: the first screen still shows the newest message, but opening it shows every message you missed from that chat (oldest at the top, newest at the bottom, scrolls). Kept: the last ~400 letters per chat.

## 1.2.3
- WhatsApp icon: removed the white outline and grey fringe, clean green bubble like the others.

## 1.2.2
- Keyboard bottom row like the Wear OS picture: ?123, a tall rounded space bar and delete side by side, with the space bar reaching down along the round edge. The full stop takes the place of the second delete key at the end of the third row (the comma is on the ?123 page).

## 1.2.1
- Keyboard: one delete key (at the end of the third row), outlined shift and delete signs like the Wear OS picture, and a wide, darker space bar next to ?123 on the bottom row. The unused big key font is removed (smaller app).

## 1.2.0
- Tapping a message opens it in full: the app's sign, the name, the whole text and a reply button. Turn the crown or swipe up and down to scroll a long text; reply opens the keyboard. Back (swipe right) goes keyboard -> message -> messages.

## 1.1.5
- The keyboard now matches the Wear OS picture: capital letters on dark rounded keys, three rows of letters with shift and delete, a bottom row with ?123, a wide space bar and delete, the ">" tile and suggestion chips above, a light-blue capsule send button, and the word being typed in light blue.
- The comma and full stop are on the ?123 page.


## 1.1.3
- Messages from the same chat (same app and name) are one page, showing the latest message; with 8 chats, a new one drops the oldest.

## 1.1.2
- The WhatsApp, Messenger and Telegram signs now look like the real icons (64 px pictures, gradient included) on the empty screen and on every message page.

## 1.1.0
- One message per page, like a phone notification: the app's sign in the middle at the top, then the sender, the time and the message in bigger letters. Swipe up/down or turn the crown to move between messages; tap to reply.
- A full-screen keyboard: big bare letters over the whole screen, quick replies above and a send button.
- Times are shown as 12:05 a.m.

## 1.0.1
- A reply counts as sent when the chat disappears on the phone as well as when the phone says so; waits 15 s.

## 1.0.0
- First version.
